by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T08:32:22Z

Thanks for your patience here. I pushed a rework rather than another round of comments, since most of it follows from dropping the rename.

The rename had to go: `_hash` is reserved by `UriSigner`, so `$uriSigner->sign($loginLink->getUrl())` threw `URI query parameter conflict: parameter name "_hash" is reserved`. Dropping it also removed the deprecation, the BC layer, and two regressions that came with the hand-rolled `getRequestParameterValue()`. That method read only `$request->query` or `$request->request`, so a route like `/login/{user}/check` stopped working, and POST stopped working. Restoring `ParameterBagUtils::getRequestParameterValue()` fixes both, and the low-deps failure goes away with the rename.

The feature is unchanged in spirit. Extra parameters are signed, the signed set is listed in `hash_parameters`, and unlisted parameters appended by mail proxies are still tolerated. Naming follows the existing `user`/`expires`/`hash`.

What I changed beyond that:

- Reserved and malformed names now throw instead of silently corrupting the link. `user`, `expires`, `hash` and `hash_parameters` are rejected, and names must match `[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+`, so a comma cannot break the listing and brackets cannot be read as a property path.
- Falsy values work. `empty()` was dropped in favour of `null` checks, so `0`, `'0'`, `''` and `false` round-trip. Booleans are normalised to `0` and `1` when signed, because that is how they travel through `http_build_query`; before this, `false` signed as an empty string and the link never validated.
- An unstringable value now reports `get_debug_type()` instead of interpolating the value, which used to emit `Array to string conversion` or fatal on an object.
- `LoginLinkHandler` and `FirewallAwareLoginLinkHandler` declare the new argument for real, so `DebugClassLoader` only warns third-party implementors.
- Added `Security` and `Security\Core` changelog entries, and moved the UPGRADE note under `Security`, since the interface lives there rather than in SecurityBundle.

Tests cover the signed round-trip, tampering, stripping, reserved and malformed names, the unstringable value, and the falsy and boolean values. Security\Http 674, Security\Core 407 and SecurityBundle 450 are green with no deprecations.

One thing I did not change, and it is worth deciding before this merges. `DefaultAuthenticationSuccessHandler::determineTargetUrl()` reads `_target_path` from the request unconditionally, so an attacker-appended `_target_path` is honoured exactly like a signed one. Signing it here does not protect it. If `_target_path` is the motivating use case, the success handler has to prefer the signed value, and `consumeLoginLink()` currently returns only a `UserInterface`, so there is no supported way for an application to read back which parameters were signed.

