by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T08:50:43Z

One more thing, since it came up while I was checking whether this feature is needed at all.

There is an alternative that works on 8.2 today: decorate `LoginLinkHandlerInterface`, append the extra parameters to the generated URL, run it through `UriSigner::sign()`, and call `UriSigner::checkRequest()` before delegating to `consumeLoginLink()`. About 25 lines. I ran it: untouched accepted, tampered value rejected, stripped parameter rejected, appended parameter rejected, and falsy values work.

It is strictly stronger than what this PR does, because `UriSigner::doVerify()` removes only `_hash` and hashes the rest of the query, so nothing can be added, removed or altered. Here, only the names listed in `hash_parameters` are covered.

I am not proposing we do that instead, because it fails in the one environment login links actually live in. Mail security products and clients routinely append tracking parameters or rewrite URLs, and any single appended parameter makes the whole `UriSigner` signature invalid. The user then gets an invalid link with nothing in the logs explaining it. Selective signing is the right answer to that, and it is the reason this PR is worth having.

But it does mean the boundary has to be documented rather than left implicit: parameters that are not declared are not protected, by design.

Which brings me back to `_target_path`. It is the example everyone will copy, and it is the parameter an attacker most wants to control. `DefaultAuthenticationSuccessHandler::determineTargetUrl()` reads it straight from the request, so passing it through `createLoginLink()` does not make it trustworthy. Two ways out:

1. the success handler prefers the signed value over the raw request parameter, which needs `consumeLoginLink()` to expose which parameters were signed, since it currently returns only a `UserInterface`
2. or we document that signing a parameter here protects it against tampering in transit, and nothing more, and we do not use `_target_path` as the example

I lean towards the first, but the second is defensible if the docs are explicit. What do you think?

