by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T14:19:44Z

Jan and I discussed the `_target_path` question out of band; recording the resolution here.

We agree that Symfony should not decide what a signed parameter means, and that the success handler must stay authenticator-agnostic. Concretely:

- The feature stays as in this PR: extra parameters are opt-in at `createLoginLink()`, signed, and validated on consume. Capturing a value (like the originally requested URL) cannot be automated anyway: links are generated in controllers handling a posted form, in message handlers or in commands, where no "current URL" exists.
- No automation for `_target_path`. Redirecting after a same-browser login link already works with no parameter at all: the target path saved in the session when the anonymous visitor hits the protected page is picked up by the success handler. In-link values only matter for cross-device clicks and stateless firewalls, and there only the app knows what to do with them.
- What was missing was a safe way to read the values back. The query string cannot tell a signed parameter from an appended one, since unlisted parameters are tolerated by design. `consumeLoginLink()` now sets the verified extra parameters on the `_login_link_parameters` request attribute; read them there.

I also considered promoting the verified values into the request so that `_target_path` handling would work transparently, and rejected it: request attributes win over query parameters everywhere `ParameterBagUtils` is used, so signed parameters could shadow routing attributes such as `_controller`. Not worth the risk.

While probing this I found three requests that crashed with a 500 instead of being rejected as an invalid link: appending `hash_parameters=x&x[]=1` to any login link, sending a signed parameter as an array (`ref[]=evil`), and listing a malformed name such as `a[b`. The consume loop now validates listed names against the same `[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+` rule used at generation and requires string values, throwing `InvalidLoginLinkException` for all three. Tests cover them.

Two similar crashes predate this PR: `expires[]=1` and a missing `user` parameter both produce a TypeError. Both exist on 6.4 already (`Request::get()` returns arrays as-is when the query uses the `foo[]=` syntax); fixed by #65422.

The description is updated accordingly and lists what the documentation should carry. From my side the open questions are resolved.
